    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "The ubiquitin proteasome system is the primary method that cells use to degrade proteins. Breakdown of this important regulatory pathway is implicated in neurodegenerative disorders, cancer, and other debilitating human diseases. The small molecule ubiquitin can be appended to its target substrate via free amino groups on lysine. Because ubiquitin itself has seven lysines, a chain of ubiquitin molecules can be linked together in thousands of different ways. Ubiquitin chains linked together at the lysine 48 site promote degradation of a tagged substrate via the 26S proteasome, whereas other linkage types, such as lysine 63 linked chains, do not promote degradation in this way. This study seeks to determine why lysine 48 linked ubiquitin chains promote degradation. One motivating hypothesis is that the chains themselves physically destabilize the tagged substrate, which makes it easier for the 26S proteasome to degrade the protein. In order to test this hypothesis, we created mutant ubiquitin monomers with only one lysine residue, rather than the seven lysine residues typically found in wild type ubiquitin. Using a protecting group strategy and in vitro biochemistry, we developed a system of stepwise ubiquitination that allowed us to create ubiquitin chains of variable linkage type and length. Lysine 48 linked chains are only pro-degradation when at least four molecules are linked together, and we were only able to create linkages of one, two, and three molecules so our hypothesis could not be fully tested in this study. Optimization of our protocol will likely increase the yield of tri-ubiquitin chains so that specific chains of greater length can be generated  in the future.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
